{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 30, :comments ["<b>The words of this song until its conclusion.</b> This is the Song of Ha'azinu that immediately follows. And the reason it is called a 'shirah' is that Israel recite it always with song and melody, and furthermore because it is written in the manner of a song. And our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted in the Sifrei: Great is the Song, for it contains matters of the present and it contains matters of the past, and it contains matters of this world and it contains matters of the world to come." "<b>And in the Midrash</b> of Tanḥuma: Why is every shirah said of the past in the feminine form, while a shirah said of the future is in the masculine form? To tell you: just as a female conceives and again conceives, so with past redemption they are redeemed and again go into exile; but in the future, once they are redeemed they never again go into exile forever, and therefore it is spoken in the masculine form, as it is said (Isaiah 26:1): ״ביום ההוא יושר השיר הזה בארץ יהודה עיר עז לנו ישועה ישית חומות וחל״, and it is written (Psalms 98:1): ״שירו לה׳ שיר חדש כי נפלאות עשה הושיעה לו ימינו וזרוע קדשו״."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:52:56.361361888Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}